    "responsePreview": "No schema in the workspace, so here's a query against a conventional `orders` table. Adjust column names to match yours.\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    order_id,\n    customer_id,\n    order_date,\n    status,\n    total_amount\nFROM orders\nWHERE customer_id = :customer_id\nORDER BY order_date DESC, order_id DESC\nLIMIT 10;\n```\n\nNotes:\n\n- `:customer_id` is a bind parameter — pass the customer's ID rather than concatenating it into the string to avoid SQL injection.\n- `order_id DESC` is a tiebreaker so results are stable when several orders share the same timestamp.\n- `LIMIT 10` works in PostgreSQL, MySQL, and SQLite. For SQL Server use `SELECT TOP 10 ...` (and drop the `LIMIT`), or for standard SQL / Oracle 12c+ use `FETCH FIRST 10 ROWS ONLY` in place of `LIMIT 10`.\n- For good performance on a large table, an index on `(customer_id, order_date DESC)` lets the database read exactly the ten rows it needs.\n\nIf you share your actual table definition, I can tailor the column names and dialect.",
